                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   “Gentlemen,” said Vale. “I’d be delighted to answer all your ques‐
tions.”
   He took out the black notebook.
   He wrote something in it. Then he looked up, past them, past the
horns and the vest and the white carpet, to the screen above the lectern,
where a line of small gray type had been waiting since 9:40.
   MOMENT OF CALM — RESUMING 10:05 PM.
   “A brief technical interruption,” said Vale, to the room. His voice
was warm and level and unafraid. “Thank you, neighbors, for your pa‐
tience. We’ll begin again in a few minutes.”
   I looked at the clock on the wall.
   9:58.
   I felt it before I understood it. A slow, enormous pressure, every‐
where at once, from far beyond the Armory walls. The coin in my
pocket had gone from hot to something I couldn’t hold. Sixty thousand
phones. Sixty-one thousand, four hundred and eight. They hadn’t been
in this room. They were in kitchens and bedrooms and on bus stops
from the river to the county line, and they were still playing rain.
   We had won a room.
   The borough was another matter.
   I steadied myself against the cold brick of the north wall, and what I
heard was not a mason. It wasn’t the Bronx, or a ledger. It came from
somewhere a long way off, through the soles of my feet and the stone
under them, through the old wall and the roof and the steel: a woman’s
voice, speaking my name, over the sound of running water.
   It was not my grandmother.
   It was Niamh, the conservator on the riverbank, whose voice I knew
from exactly one phone call.
   And behind her, I could hear others.




